\title{Joint Capacity of Dial Batteries:\\
Compounding Synergy, Two Ceilings, and Purely Higher-Order Information}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a finitary, counting-based theory of the information that a \emph{battery} of residue dials carries about a hidden label on a finite population, and use it to analyse a measured phenomenon we call \emph{synergy compounding}. For a four-dial battery of pairwise-coprime moduli $31,23,9,8$ --- jointly, the Chinese-Remainder code of modulus $51{,}336$ --- read against a shared population of thirty thousand semiprimes, the joint capacity is $I = 8.2246$ bits while the additive prediction obtained by summing the four single-dial capacities is only $\Sigma = 3.9099$ bits. The synergy $I-\Sigma = +4.3147$ bits therefore exceeds the additive prediction itself, and the joint reading comes within $1.3$ bits of the label-entropy ceiling $H(L) = 9.5276$ bits. Decomposed by order, the total synergy at order two (six pairs) is $+0.244$ bits, at order three (four triples) $+3.822$ bits, and at order four $+4.315$ bits: pairwise interactions carry $6\%$ of the effect. On the theoretical side we derive a log-sum inequality in counting form, an exact cell decomposition of empirical conditional entropy, and a data processing inequality for empirical mutual information; from these we obtain vanishing capacity of the empty battery, monotonicity of joint capacity, the label-entropy ceiling, the code ceilings $\info(S) \le \log_2\prod_{i\in S} m_i$ and $\info(S) \le \log_2|\Omega|$, two-sided synergy bounds, and a synergy budget. We then prove that for the $k$-dial parity battery on $\{0,1\}^k$ every proper sub-battery carries exactly $0$ bits while the full battery carries exactly $1$ bit, its ceiling; consequently no inequality $\info(S) \le c\sum_{i\in S}\info(\{i\})$ holds for any constant $c$, and no control of synergies of order below $k$ constrains the joint synergy.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} mutual information, synergy, higher-order interaction, Chinese Remainder Theorem, data processing inequality, empirical entropy, parity, feature selection.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}

Suppose a hidden structure on a finite population is probed by several coarse measurements, each individually almost uninformative. How much does the ensemble know? The naive answer --- add up what each probe knows --- is not even an inequality: ensembles can know far more than the sum of their parts, and can also know less.

The motivating setting is arithmetic. Let $\Omega$ be a population of semiprimes $N=pq$. A \emph{dial} of modulus $m$ reads $N \bmod m$; a \emph{battery} is a finite family of dials, and by the Chinese Remainder Theorem a battery of pairwise-coprime moduli $m_1,\dots,m_k$ is the single dial of modulus $M = \prod_i m_i$. The hidden \emph{label} is a function of the factors: for each modulus, the unordered pair $\{p \bmod m_i,\ q \bmod m_i\}$.

\subsection{The measurement}

On one shared population of thirty thousand semiprimes, with $m = (31,23,9,8)$ and $M = 51{,}336$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lr}
\toprule
quantity & measured value \\
\midrule
joint capacity $I$ & $8.2246$ bits\\
additive prediction $\Sigma$ (four marginals) & $3.9099$ bits\\
synergy $I-\Sigma$ & $+4.3147$ bits\\
label-entropy ceiling $H(L)$ & $9.5276$ bits\\
order-2 total synergy (six pairs) & $+0.244$ bits\\
order-3 total synergy (four triples) & $+3.822$ bits\\
order-4 synergy (the battery) & $+4.315$ bits\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Two features stand out: the joint capacity \emph{more than doubles} the additive prediction ($2\Sigma = 7.8198 < 8.2246$), and the synergy is genuinely higher order, the pairwise table accounting for $0.244/4.315 \approx 5.7\%$ of the total. The mechanism is structural: one dial exposes one residue of the product, consistent with many residue pairs of the factors, whereas the joint Chinese-Remainder code exposes all four residues simultaneously ($\log_2 51{,}336 \approx 15.65$ binary units of distinguishing power), against which each dial's residue-pair label becomes nearly determined. Determination is a joint event and has no pairwise shadow.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item A finitary mutual-information calculus on empirical populations (Section~\ref{sec:calculus}).
\item Capacity arithmetic for batteries: monotonicity, ceilings, synergy bounds, the budget (Section~\ref{sec:arith}).
\item Two witnesses that synergy is genuinely higher order (Section~\ref{sec:witness}).
\item Consistency certificates for the measured table and an honest sparse-table error analysis (Sections~\ref{sec:cert}--\ref{sec:bias}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Setting and definitions}

Throughout, $\Omega$ is a finite non-empty population with $N=|\Omega|$, and all statistics are functions out of $\Omega$ with finite image. Logarithms written $\log$ are natural; quantities in \emph{bits} are divided by $\log 2$. All quantities are empirical (plug-in), computed from counts with $\Omega$ uniformly weighted.

\begin{definition}[Counts and empirical entropy]
For $f : \Omega \to A$ and a value $a$, let $c_f(a) = |f^{-1}(a)|$ and let $\img(f)$ be the set of attained values. The \emph{empirical entropy} is
\[
H(f) \;=\; \sum_{a \in \img(f)} \frac{c_f(a)}{N}\bigl(\log N - \log c_f(a)\bigr),
\]
and $H_2(f) = H(f)/\log 2$.
\end{definition}

Two facts are used constantly: $H(g\circ f) \le H(f)$ for any post-processing $g$, and $H$ depends only on the partition of $\Omega$ into fibres. If $f$ takes $m$ values with all fibres of size $k$, then $H(f) = \log m = \log N - \log k$.

\begin{definition}[Conditional entropy and trace information]
For a label $L : \Omega \to \Lambda$ and $f : \Omega \to A$, write $(L,f) : x \mapsto (L(x),f(x))$. Then
\[
H(L\mid f) = H\bigl((L,f)\bigr) - H(f), \qquad
I(L;f) = H(L) - H(L\mid f) = H(L) + H(f) - H\bigl((L,f)\bigr),
\]
and $I_2(L;f) = I(L;f)/\log 2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Dial, battery, joint reading]
A \emph{dial} is a modulus $m \ge 1$ together with a reading $r : \Omega \to \{0,\dots,m-1\}$. A \emph{battery} is a family $d = (d_i)_{i \in \iota}$ of dials. For finite $S \subseteq \iota$ the \emph{joint reading} is $r_S : x \mapsto (r_i(x))_{i\in S}$ and the \emph{code entropy} is $\cpc(S) = H_2(r_S)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Joint capacity, synergy, pairwise total]
\[
\info(S) = I_2(L; r_S), \qquad
\syn(S) = \info(S) - \sum_{i\in S}\info(\{i\}), \qquad
\pairsyn(S) = \sum_{\substack{T\subseteq S\\ |T| = 2}} \syn(T).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
$\syn$ compares against \emph{single-dial} marginals rather than a M\"obius interaction decomposition; this is the quantity reported by the experiment and needs no inclusion--exclusion over a lattice. ``Order-$k$ total synergy'' means $\sum_{|T|=k}\syn(T)$.
\end{remark}

\section{A finitary mutual-information calculus}\label{sec:calculus}

\begin{theorem}[Log-sum inequality]\label{thm:logsum}
Let $t$ be a finite index set, $a_i \ge 0$, $b_i > 0$. Then
\[
\sum_{i\in t} a_i(\log b_i - \log a_i) \;\le\; \Bigl(\sum_{i\in t} a_i\Bigr)\Bigl(\log\sum_{i\in t} b_i - \log\sum_{i\in t} a_i\Bigr),
\]
with $0\cdot\log 0 = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Put $A = \sum a_i$, $B = \sum b_i$. If $A=0$ both sides vanish. Otherwise $B>0$; for $a_i>0$ apply $\log t \le t-1$ at $t = b_iA/(a_iB)$ to get
$\log b_i - \log a_i - (\log B - \log A) \le \frac{b_iA}{a_iB} - 1$, and multiply by $a_i$:
\[
a_i(\log b_i - \log a_i) \;\le\; a_i(\log B - \log A) + \Bigl(\tfrac{A}{B}b_i - a_i\Bigr),
\]
which also holds trivially when $a_i = 0$. Summing, the corrections telescope to $\tfrac{A}{B}B - A = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
For a population of size $N$, a cell of size $c$ inside a block of size $C$ contributes $\psi(N;c,C) = \frac{c}{N}(\log C - \log c)$.
\end{definition}

Writing $\varphi(N;c) = \frac{c}{N}(\log N - \log c)$ one has $\psi(N;c,C) = \varphi(N;c) + \frac{c}{N}(\log C - \log N)$ and hence, for counts $c_i$ summing to $C$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:split}
\sum_i \psi(N;c_i,C) \;=\; \Bigl(\sum_i \varphi(N;c_i)\Bigr) - \varphi(N;C),
\end{equation}
i.e.\ the conditional-entropy contribution of a block is the entropy defect created by splitting it into cells.

\begin{theorem}[Counting log-sum inequality]\label{thm:psisum}
For counts $c_i \ge 0$ and $C_i > 0$,
$\sum_i \psi(N;c_i,C_i) \le \psi\bigl(N;\sum_i c_i, \sum_i C_i\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:logsum} with $a_i = c_i$, $b_i = C_i$, scaled by $1/N$.
\end{proof}

Interpretation: merging cells can only raise the conditional-entropy contribution.

\begin{theorem}[Cell decomposition]\label{thm:cells}
$\displaystyle H(L\mid f) = \sum_{a\in\img(f)}\ \sum_{\ell\in\img(L)} \psi\bigl(N;\,c_{(L,f)}(\ell,a),\,c_f(a)\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $H((L,f))$ as a double sum of $\varphi$-contributions; for each $a$ the joint counts sum over $\ell$ to the marginal $c_f(a)$, so \eqref{eq:split} applies blockwise. Subtracting $H(f) = \sum_a \varphi(N;c_f(a))$ leaves the stated sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Data processing for conditional entropy]\label{thm:dpi}
For any post-processing $g$, $H(L\mid f) \le H(L \mid g\circ f)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Group $a \in \img(f)$ by $b = g(a)$. Then $c_{(L,g\circ f)}(\ell,b) = \sum_{a: g(a)=b} c_{(L,f)}(\ell,a)$ and $c_{g\circ f}(b) = \sum_{a:g(a)=b} c_f(a)$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:psisum} inside each group and each $\ell$, then sum; by Theorem~\ref{thm:cells} the two sides reassemble to the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Data processing for trace information]\label{cor:dpi}
$I(L; g\circ f) \le I(L;f)$, and likewise in bits.
\end{corollary}

This is what makes joint capacity monotone in the battery; it is \emph{not} a consequence of $H(g\circ f)\le H(f)$, which says only that the coarsened code is smaller.

\begin{theorem}[Capacity depends only on the partition]\label{thm:partition}
If $f(x) = f(y) \iff g(x) = g(y)$ for all $x,y$, then $H(f)=H(g)$ and $I(L;f) = I(L;g)$ for every $L$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each of $f,g$ factors through the other, so entropy monotonicity applies in both directions; the same for the paired statistics, and $I = H(L)+H(\cdot)-H((L,\cdot))$.
\end{proof}

This licenses replacing a Chinese-Remainder residue vector by the single integer $N \bmod M$, or any injective code of it.

\begin{theorem}[Determination attains the ceiling]\label{thm:det}
If $f(x)=f(y) \Rightarrow L(x)=L(y)$, then $I(L;f) = H(L)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Then $(L,f)$ and $f$ induce the same partition, so $H((L,f)) = H(f)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Incremental bound]\label{thm:incr}
$I\bigl(L;(f,g)\bigr) \le I(L;f) + H(g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine $H((f,g)) \le H(f)+H(g)$ with $H((L,f)) \le H\bigl((L,(f,g))\bigr)$.
\end{proof}

\section{Capacity arithmetic for batteries}\label{sec:arith}

Fix a battery $d$ with moduli $m_i$ and a label $L$.

\begin{theorem}[Nonnegativity]
$\info(S) \ge 0$ for every $S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The empty battery carries nothing]
$\info(\emptyset) = 0$, hence $\syn(\emptyset)=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The empty reading is constant, so $H(r_\emptyset)=0$ and $(L,r_\emptyset)$ induces the partition of $L$; thus $I = H(L)+0-H(L)=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotone capacity]\label{thm:mono}
If $S \subseteq T$ then $\info(S) \le \info(T)$; in particular $\info(\{i\}) \le \info(S)$ for $i \in S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$r_S = \rho \circ r_T$ for the coordinate restriction $\rho$; apply Corollary~\ref{cor:dpi}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Label-entropy ceiling]\label{thm:labceil}
$\info(S) \le H_2(L)$ for every $S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Code ceilings]\label{thm:codeceil}
For every $S$,
\[
\info(S) \;\le\; \cpc(S) \;\le\; \log_2\prod_{i\in S} m_i,
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\info(S) \;\le\; \log_2 N .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$I(L;f) \le H(f)$ since $H(L) \le H((L,f))$; a joint reading attains at most $\prod_{i\in S}m_i$ values, and at most $N$ of them are occupied, while entropy is at most the logarithm of the number of occupied values.
\end{proof}

For the measured battery, $\log_2 51{,}336 \approx 15.647$ bits, and with $N = 30{,}000$ the sparse-table ceiling is $\approx 14.87$ bits.

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided synergy bounds]
For every $S$,
\[
\max_{i\in S}\Bigl(\info(\{i\}) - \sum_{j\in S}\info(\{j\})\Bigr) \;\le\; \syn(S) \;\le\; H_2(L).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Upper: Theorem~\ref{thm:labceil} plus nonnegativity of the marginals. Lower: Theorem~\ref{thm:mono} and subtraction.
\end{proof}

A battery of $k$ dials all carrying the \emph{same} $c$ bits has $\syn = -(k-1)c$, meeting the lower bound: redundancy is bounded, whereas super-additivity is bounded only by the label entropy.

\begin{theorem}[Synergy budget]\label{thm:budget}
$\displaystyle \syn(S) \le \sum_{i\in S}\bigl(\cpc(\{i\}) - \info(\{i\})\bigr)$, where $\cpc(\{i\}) = H_2(r_i)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Sub-additivity gives $\cpc(S) \le \sum_{i\in S}\cpc(\{i\})$; combine with $\info(S) \le \cpc(S)$ and subtract the marginal sum.
\end{proof}

Synergy is paid for out of unused code capacity: a dial already saturated as a label predictor has nothing left to contribute jointly. For the measured battery the budget is at most $15.647 - 3.9099 \approx 11.74$ bits against a measured $+4.3147$.

\begin{theorem}[Pure-synergy regime]
If $\info(\{i\})=0$ for all $i \in S$ then $\syn(S) = \info(S)$, and $\syn(S) > 0$ as soon as the battery carries anything.
\end{theorem}

\section{Higher-order synergy is real: two witnesses}\label{sec:witness}

\subsection{The three-dial parity witness}

Let $\Omega = \{0,1\}^3$ ($N=8$), let the battery be the three coordinate dials of modulus $2$, and let $L(x) = x_1\oplus x_2\oplus x_3$.

\begin{theorem}[Capacity entirely of order three]\label{thm:par3}
\begin{enumerate}
\item $H_2(L) = 1$;
\item $\info(\{i\}) = 0$ for each $i$;
\item $\info(S) = 0$ for every $S$ with $|S|=2$, hence every pairwise synergy vanishes and $\pairsyn = 0$;
\item $\info(\{1,2,3\}) = 1 = H_2(L)$ and $\syn(\{1,2,3\}) = 1$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
All statistics involved have equal-size fibres, so each entropy is the logarithm of the number of attained cells. $H(L)=\log 2$ and $H(r_i) = \log 2$, while $(L,r_i)$ attains four cells of size two, $H = \log 4$; hence $I = \log2+\log2-\log4 = 0$. For distinct $i,j$, $r_{\{i,j\}}$ attains four cells of size two and $(L,r_{\{i,j\}})$ separates all eight individuals, so $I = \log2+\log4-\log8 = 0$. All three coordinates determine the individual, hence the label, so Theorem~\ref{thm:det} gives $I = H(L)$, one bit.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
No inequality $\info(S) \le c\sum_{i\in S}\info(\{i\})$ holds for any constant $c$ over all populations, labels and batteries.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
No bound on pairwise synergies constrains the joint synergy: here all pairwise synergies vanish while the joint synergy is at the label-entropy ceiling.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The $k$-dial parity battery}

Fix $k \ge 1$, let $\Omega = \{0,1\}^k$, let dial $i$ read bit $i$, and let $L(x) = \sum_i x_i \bmod 2$.

\begin{theorem}[Blindness of every proper sub-battery]\label{thm:park}
For every $S \subsetneq \{1,\dots,k\}$ one has $\info(S) = 0$, while $\info(\{1,\dots,k\}) = 1 = H_2(L)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The fibre of $r_S$ through $a$ is the agreement subcube $\{x : x_i = a_i\ \forall i\in S\}$, of size $2^{\,k-|S|}$; hence $H(r_S) = |S|\log 2$. If $S$ is proper, choose $j \notin S$; the bit-flip involution $\sigma_j$ preserves each agreement class and toggles the parity, so it matches the even- and odd-parity halves of the class exactly. Thus all fibres of $(L,r_S)$ have size $2^{\,k-|S|-1}$ and $H((L,r_S)) = (|S|+1)\log 2$, giving
\[
I(L;r_S) = \log 2 + |S|\log 2 - (|S|+1)\log 2 = 0 .
\]
For $S$ full, the reading determines the individual and Theorem~\ref{thm:det} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Synergy of pure order $k$]
For $k \ge 2$: every dial carries $0$ bits, every sub-battery of width $<k$ has capacity and synergy $0$, and the full battery has capacity $1$ bit --- its ceiling --- all of it order-$k$ synergy. Hence no bookkeeping over sub-batteries of bounded order can predict joint capacity.
\end{corollary}

Batteries thus form a scale: redundant ones with synergy down to $-(k-1)\max_i\info(\{i\})$; additive ones with synergy $0$; parity-like ones with all marginals $0$ and all capacity at top order. The measured semiprime battery sits far towards the parity end without reaching the extreme.

\section{Consistency of the measured table}\label{sec:cert}

\begin{proposition}
The measured table satisfies:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $8.2246 \le 9.5276$: below the label-entropy ceiling, deficit $1.3030$ bits;
\item $8.2246 \le \log_2 51{,}336$, since $\log_2 51{,}336 \ge \log_2 2^{15} = 15$;
\item $8.2246 - 3.9099 = 4.3147$;
\item $2\cdot 3.9099 = 7.8198 < 8.2246$: the joint capacity more than doubles the additive prediction;
\item $0.244 < 0.06\cdot 4.3147 = 0.2589$: the pairwise total is under $6\%$ of the joint synergy;
\item $4.3147 \le \log_2 51{,}336 - 3.9099$: the synergy fits inside the budget of Theorem~\ref{thm:budget}.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\subsection{An exact companion computation}

Because the theory is finitary it can also be evaluated \emph{exactly} on a population enumerated in full, where no estimation question arises. Take all $34{,}191$ semiprimes $N=pq$ with $1000 < p < q < 3000$, the same four dials, and as hidden label the smaller prime factor $p$. Then, exactly:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lr}
\toprule
quantity & value (bits)\\
\midrule
marginal capacities of the four dials & $0.0574,\ 0.0585,\ 0.0157,\ 0.0013$\\
additive prediction $\Sigma$ & $0.1328$\\
joint capacity $I$ & $6.2828$\\
synergy $I-\Sigma$ & $+6.1500$ (a factor $47$ over $\Sigma$)\\
label-entropy ceiling $H_2(L)$ & $7.7520$\\
order-2 total synergy (six pairs) & $+3.5588$ (mean $+0.5931$)\\
order-3 total synergy (four triples) & $+12.1119$ (mean $+3.0280$)\\
order-4 synergy & $+6.1500$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Every structural inequality of Sections~\ref{sec:calculus}--\ref{sec:arith} holds, and the qualitative picture is the reported one in sharper form: negligible marginals, per-sub-battery synergy growing steeply with order ($0.59 \to 3.03 \to 6.15$ bits), and joint capacity within $1.47$ bits of the ceiling. A single residue of the product constrains the factor pair hardly at all, while the joint code, with $15.65$ bits of resolution, nearly pins the product and hence the factorisation.

\section{Sparse-table bias and an honest caveat}\label{sec:bias}

The same experiment reports a \emph{which-factor} statistic at $0.0469$ bits on the full joint code, above every pairwise threshold. We do not claim it as signal. Plug-in mutual information is biased upward on sparse contingency tables: with $R$ occupied reading-columns and $|\Lambda|$ label values on $N$ samples, the plug-in estimate exceeds the truth by roughly $\frac{(R-1)(|\Lambda|-1)}{2N\log 2}$ bits even when the true value is zero. Here $R$ may be as large as $51{,}336$ against $N = 30{,}000$: most columns hold zero or one individual, and a singleton column is tautologically predictive of that individual's label. The sparse-table ceiling $\info(S) \le \log_2 N$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:codeceil} says precisely that all such ``information'' is bounded by the sample's own naming entropy.

The appropriate reading is therefore \emph{suspected plug-in bias}, and the substantive factor-blindness claim rests on the well-conditioned strata --- sub-batteries whose code size is small relative to $N$ --- together with the ceiling analysis. The design rule is: a battery whose code entropy exceeds $\log_2 N$ cannot be evaluated honestly at full resolution on $N$ samples; enlarge the population, coarsen the code (which by data processing yields a valid lower bound), or use a bias-corrected estimator.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Empirical joint capacity.} Given the population, the label and readings $r_i$, $i \in S$, build hash tables of counts for $r_S$, for $L$ and for $(L,r_S)$, and return $\bigl(H(L)+H(r_S)-H((L,r_S))\bigr)/\log 2$. Cost: $O(N|S|)$ time, $O(\min(N,\prod_{i\in S}m_i))$ space; only occupied cells are ever materialised.

\paragraph{Order decomposition.} Compute the $k$ marginals once, then for each order $j$ iterate over the $\binom{k}{j}$ subsets, computing $\info(T)$ and subtracting the marginal sum. Cost $O(2^kNk)$ overall; fifteen passes for $k=4$.

\paragraph{Ceiling audit.} Check monotonicity, the label-entropy ceiling, the code and sparse-table ceilings, the two-sided synergy bounds and the budget. Each is a theorem, so a violation is proof of an implementation bug.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{Feature and sensor selection.} Ranking candidates by individual informativeness can fail totally: in the parity battery every candidate scores zero and the optimal set is all of them. Theorem~\ref{thm:budget} provides a cheap screening rule --- sets whose members are individually near-saturated cannot hide much synergy.

\paragraph{Auditing information leakage.} Per-channel audits are unsound: each channel may certify at zero leakage while the ensemble leaks everything. Monotonicity turns coarse audits into valid lower bounds, and the ceilings give upper bounds with no measurement at all.

\paragraph{Arithmetic statistics.} Each added coprime modulus multiplies the number of distinguishable columns and shrinks the deficit against the label ceiling; with four dials the deficit is $1.30$ bits out of $9.53$.

\section{Discussion}

The revision forced on the naive picture is one of scale and order. A pairwise table suggests a mild correction to additive bookkeeping; the measured decomposition shows the correction exceeds the thing it corrects, with almost all of it above order two. The parity witnesses show this is not a quirk: for every width $k$ there are batteries whose capacity is entirely of order $k$, so the pairwise correction can be exactly zero while the joint effect is maximal.

What survives is only what is proved: capacity starts at zero, grows monotonically, and is bounded by the label entropy, the joint code entropy, $\log_2 N$, and in its synergy by unused code capacity. Those five facts are the whole universal theory. The parity examples do not claim that real batteries have zero marginals; they show that no theorem can bound joint capacity by marginals or low-order interactions, so the observed high-order share cannot be argued away by any general principle.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Ceiling-approach law]
For a battery of pairwise-coprime moduli on a population with label entropy $H_2(L)$, if each added dial separates a constant fraction of the currently confused pairs then
\[
H_2(L) - \info(S\cup\{i\}) \;\le\; (1-c)\bigl(H_2(L) - \info(S)\bigr).
\]
\end{conjecture}

The deficit is exactly $H_2(L\mid r_S)$ and each new dial refines the fibre partition, so a uniform separation rate should convert into uniform multiplicative decay --- a Doob-style martingale contraction in a finitary setting. Monotone decay is available (Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi}); only the rate is missing, and $8.2246$ against a ceiling of $9.5276$ after four dials measures that rate.

\begin{conjecture}[Order-profile rigidity]
For every $k$ and every profile $(s_2,\dots,s_k)$ of nonnegative reals with $\sum_r s_r \le H_2(L)$, there is a battery of $k$ binary dials whose total synergy at order $r$ equals $s_r$ for each $r$.
\end{conjecture}

Further directions: bias-corrected joint estimation with confidence intervals degrading gracefully in $R/N$; and optimal battery design --- given $\prod_i m_i \le M$, which coprime multiset maximises joint capacity for a given arithmetic label?

\section{Conclusion}

A battery of coarse dials is not the sum of its dials. Four residue dials contributing $3.9099$ bits individually contribute $8.2246$ bits jointly --- a synergy of $+4.3147$ bits, more than doubling the additive prediction and within $1.3$ bits of the $9.5276$-bit label-entropy ceiling --- with pairwise interactions accounting for only $6\%$ of that synergy. Joint capacity vanishes on the empty battery, is monotone, and is capped by the label entropy, the joint code entropy, $\log_2 N$ and the unused-code budget; beyond that nothing general is true, since parity batteries of every width are blind on every proper sub-battery and saturate their ceiling on the whole. Capacity must be computed jointly; marginal bookkeeping is not an approximation to it, but a different quantity.

